ht\\\": 0.0}` - that's inside the JSON string. The backslash before double quote is part of JSON escaping. The actual Python code will have backslashes? No, when the JSON is parsed, the backslash-quote becomes a literal double quote in the string. So the Python code will contain `return {\"mst_edges\": [], \"total_weight\": 0.0}`. That's correct.\n\nAlso note: The Python code has `if n <= 1:\\n        return {\\\"mst_edges\\\": [], \\\"total_weight\\\": 0.0}` - note the indentation: after colon, newline, then spaces. In the JSON, the newline is represented as \\n, then spaces. That will preserve indentation. Good.\n\nNow, produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2050,"completion_tokens":5565,"total_tokens":7615,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":5081},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":2},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}